&lt;Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) is a technique used to control the amount of power delivered to an electrical device by rapidly switching it on and off. The ratio of the time the signal is on versus off determines the average power delivered, which can be adjusted to control brightness, speed, or other parameters.

In embedded systems, PWM is implemented using a timer peripheral that generates a square wave with adjustable duty cycle. The duty cycle is the percentage of time the signal is high during one full cycle. For example, a 50% duty cycle means the signal is on for half the time and off for the other half.

Two distinct applications of PWM are:

- <strong>Controlling LED brightness:</strong> By varying the duty cycle of the PWM signal connected to an LED, the perceived brightness can be adjusted. A higher duty cycle makes the LED brighter, while a lower one makes it dimmer. This is commonly used in backlighting for displays or ambient lighting systems.

- <strong>Driving a motor at variable speed:</strong> PWM is used to control the effective voltage applied to a DC motor by varying the duty cycle of the signal sent to its power supply. A higher duty cycle increases the motor's speed, while a lower one decreases it. This allows for smooth and efficient speed control in robotics or electric vehicles.

Here is an example of how PWM might be configured on a microcontroller using C code:

```c
// Example: Configure PWM on a STM32 microcontroller
void configure_pwm(void) {
    // Enable the clock for the timer peripheral (TIM2)
    RCC_APB1PeriphClockCmd(RCC_APB1Periph_TIM2, ENABLE);

    // Set up the timer to run at 1 MHz
    TIM_TimeBaseInitTypeDef TIM_TimeBaseStruct;
    TIM_TimeBaseStruct.TIM_Prescaler = 7199;          // 84 MHz / (7199 + 1) = 11.5 kHz
    TIM_TimeBaseStruct.TIM_Period = 999;              // 11.5 kHz / 1000 = 11.5 Hz
    TIM_TimeBaseStruct.TIM_CounterMode = TIM_CounterMode_Up;
    TIM_TimeBaseInit(TIM2, &amp;TIM_TimeBaseStruct);

    // Set the PWM channel (e.g., channel 1)
    TIM_OCInitTypeDef TIM_OCStruct;
    TIM_OCStruct.TIM_OCMode = TIM_OCMode_PWM1;        // PWM mode
    TIM_OCStruct.TIM_OutputState = TIM_OutputState_Enable;
    TIM_OCStruct.TIM_Pulse = 500;                     // 50% duty cycle (500/1000)
    TIM_OCStruct.TIM_OCPolarity = TIM_OCPolarity_High;

    TIM_OC1Init(TIM2, &amp;TIM_OCStruct);                 // Initialize channel 1
    TIM_OC1PreloadConfig(TIM2, TIM_OCPreload_Enable); // Enable preload

    // Start the timer
    TIM_Cmd(TIM2, ENABLE);
}
```

In this example, the `TIM_Pulse` value determines the duty cycle. A value of 500 out of a total period of 1000 gives a 50% duty cycle. This setup allows for precise control over devices like LEDs or motors.

A <strong>mutex</strong> (short for "mutual exclusion") solves the problem of race conditions by ensuring that only one thread can access a shared resource at a time. A <strong>race condition</strong> occurs when two or more threads try to modify the same data simultaneously, leading to unpredictable and incorrect results.

A <strong>critical section</strong> is a part of the code where a shared resource (like a variable or hardware register) is accessed. It must be protected so that only one thread can execute it at any given moment. Without protection, multiple threads could read and write to the same data concurrently, causing inconsistencies.

Here's how a mutex works:

- When a thread wants to enter a critical section, it attempts to acquire the mutex.
- If the mutex is already locked by another thread, the requesting thread waits until it becomes available.
- Once the mutex is acquired, the thread can safely access the shared resource.
- After finishing with the resource, the thread releases the mutex, allowing other threads to proceed.

This ensures that only one thread is in the critical section at a time, preventing race conditions.

```c
#include &lt;pthread.h&gt;

// Shared variable
int shared_data = 0;

// Mutex for protecting access to shared_data
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void* increment_data(void* arg) {
    // Acquire the mutex before entering the critical section
    pthread_mutex_lock(&amp;lock);

    // Critical section: modify shared data
    shared_data++;

    // Release the mutex after leaving the critical section
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;lock);

    return NULL;
}
```

In this example, `pthread_mutex_lock` and `pthread_mutex_unlock` protect the increment operation on `shared_data`. Even if multiple threads call `increment_data`, only one will be allowed to modify `shared_data` at a time.
